   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the uniform commercial code, in relation to modernizing commercial law in New York state; and to repeal certain provisions of such code relating thereto",
    "basePrintNo": "S7816",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7816-2013",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Rpld Arts 1 & 7, §2-A--207, amd UCC, generally",
    "lawSection": "Uniform Commercial Code",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S7816\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the uniform commercial code, in\nrelation to modernizing commercial law in New York state; and to\nrepeal certain provisions of such code relating thereto\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo modernize several sections of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) and\nbring New York up to date with comparable revisions that have been\nenacted in other states.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill repeals existing Article 1 of the UCC and enacts\na new Article 1 (\"General Provisions\").\n\nSections 2 through 13 make technical and clarifying amendments within\nArticle 2 of the UCC.\n\nSections 14 through 22 make renumbering and clarifying amendments\nwithin Article 2-A of the UCC.\n\nSection 23 repeals existing Article 7 of the UCC and enacts a new\nArticle 7 (\"Documents of Title\").\n\nSections 24 and 25 make clarifying amendments within Article 8 of the\nUCC.\n\nSections 26 through 50 make amendments within Article 9 of the UCC\n\n(\"Secured Transactions\").\n\nSection 51 provides for an immediate effective date, and specifies\nthat it shall apply to transactions entered into on or after such\ndate.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill would modernize three articles of the Uniform Commercial\nCode (UCC) and bring New York up to date with comparable revisions\nenacted in other states. Historically, New York has been a leading\nstate for commercial law and this status generates substantial\neconomic activity and employment in the State of New York.\nUnfortunately, New York has fallen significantly behind its sister\nstates in modernizing its commercial law.\n\nArticle 1 contains definitions and concepts which apply to all\narticles of the UCC and impact every transaction governed by the UCC.\nArticle 1 needs to be updated to reflect the fact that other articles\nof the UCC have been revised and amended to accommodate changing\nbusiness practices and developments in the law. About forty states\nhave enacted Revised Article 1 of the UCC and the revision is\ncurrently pending in some other states.\n\nArticle 7 governs \"documents of title,\" items such as bills of lading\nand warehouse receipts that control the right to the goods described\n\n\ntherein during commercial storage and shipment. New York's current\nArticle 7 does not recognize electronic documents of title, and\ntherefore does not permit goods covered by electronic documents to be\ntransferred or financed. The revisions to Article 7 are consistent\nwith the increasing recognition of electronic documents and\ninstruments as equal in status to their paper counterparts under both\nNew York and federal law. About forty states have also enacted Revised\nArticle 7 of the UCC and the revision is currently pending in some\nother states.\n\nArticle 9 governs security interest in personal property. The Article\nwas substantially revised and enacted in New York in 2001. The current\nbill contains targeted amendments (the \"Amendments\") to Article 9\nrelating to the rules for the system for filing and searching\nfinancing statements, other discrete changes and some transition\nrules. At least eight states have enacted the 2010 Amendments to\nArticle 9 of the UCC and the amendments are currently pending in\nseveral other states.\n\nThe National Conference of Commissioners on Uniform State Laws\n(NCCUSL) has promulgated model revisions for Articles 1 and 7 which\ncompletely rewrite these articles of the Uniform Commercial Code.\nNCCUSL has also promulgated significant amendments for Article 9 of\nthe Uniform Commercial Code. The New York City Bar Association led\nthis effort to revise and update these articles. With a few\nexceptions, the Article 1 and 7 revisions and the amendments to\nArticle 9 reflect the draft from the Bar.\n\nEnactment of these revisions and amendments will make commercial\ntransactions more predictable and will bring New York Law into accord\nwith many other states.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nImmediate.\n\n",
